Z||000016||Black88GT||09-14-2001||08:52 PM||||We had to help Afghanistan to get back at the Soviets for helping communist Vietnam.  We gave them stinger missles to take out the HINDs (flying tank pretty much, slow but deadly).  <P>Afghanistan was their Vietnam.  They didn't commit 100% to the war.  Then Afghanistan started with the stingers and they had our financial backing, mounting pressure from the rest of the world finally killed the operation.  <P>They have no financial backing.  How will they get supplies?  They have Cold war era technology.  We know it like the back of our hands.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Sootah||09-14-2001||09:20 PM||||I also am 19. If there does happen to be a draft, then I am going to go join the air force. If I have to join the military, then I am going to do it on my terms. I don't want to be an infantry unit, special forces (halo, prefferably) is the only thing like that I would consider.<P>So, assuming there is, what service should I go into? I'm not all that afraid of dying, I am, however, afraid of being mangled beyond belife.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif">   <BR><I>Never pull your pants down in Cosco, the dang monkeys will getcha</I> <BR><B>AIM</B>: KSSouter||||reg||1
Z||000018||fiero56||09-14-2001||10:12 PM||||First I heard about this I was shocked as it was happening. Then wondered through the day if anything 'big' was going to happen, near me. I've been saddened about thus until today. Now I'm downright pi$$ed that a group of people felt they could pull this off and  get away. If someone had asked me to join the forces I would have laughed(I don't need to go fight in some foreign country to die for someone elses causes that mean little to me), but when it involves my country, and our people and properties, sign me up! If there were a draft I would want to be one of the first over there.<P>I'm 21, in college, unmarried, and have no kids. Give me the training I need and send me out.<P>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 09-14-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 09-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||johnt671||09-15-2001||08:08 AM||||They don't need much in the line of supplies as they will live of the land and have the support of the people.  They are used to having nothing.  They use to use to trap Russian armored convoys in the mountians by disabling the first and last vechiles in the convoy in spots were they couldn't turn around, pile up wood on the tanks while snipers kept the tanks buttoned up and set them on fire.  If the crews tried to escape the shot them.  This was done with those old weapons they were shown with.  As the war progressed they had weapons they took from the Russians and if they needed anything else to support them the took that also.  The only thing the had real trouble with was the choppers.  Of course most of this will be overcome if we fight the war right.  My concern is that we still rely on bombs when we know they are not all that effective (mpressive though).      ||||reg||1
Z||000020||RoadRocket||09-15-2001||11:18 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by johnt671:<BR><B>I can't see the draft starting up but a good point raised was how tough the Afgans fought the Russians,  who have a lot fewer rules of warfare than we do.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The rules are changing. Wars were fought to destroy a nations ability to make war. Afghanistans ability to make war lies in its people (terrorism) not in its industry. Its gonna get bloody and ugly, but it has to be done as Tuesday was not likely the end of this. <P><P>------------------<BR>Ed Dana<BR>88 Coupe.||||reg||1
